{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","title":"def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI.md","line":69,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI.tex#def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI.tex","label":"def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","line":205,"source":"\\begin{definition}[parallel field along a curve]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch6-parallel-field}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0252}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve}\n\n  A vector field $V$ along $c$ is \\emph{parallel along $c$} if $\\dot V\\equiv0$.\n  The tangent field $\\dot c$ along a geodesic is parallel, and (by\n  \\cref{ex:pet-ch6-sphere-third-partials}) the unit field perpendicular to a\n  great circle in $S^2(1)$ is parallel. If $V,W$ are parallel along $c$ then\n  $g(V,W)$ is constant along $c$ by the product rule, so parallel fields neither\n  change length nor relative angle, generalizing parallel translation in\n  Euclidean space.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[parallel field along a curve] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368362d706172616c6c656c2d6669656c64\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0252</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI%23def%3Apet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve\">↗ def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve</a> <p></p>A vector field <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$V$\">$V$</span> along <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$c$\">$c$</span> is <em>parallel along <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$c$\">$c$</span></em> if <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\dot V\\equiv 0$\">$\\dot V\\equiv 0$</span>. The tangent field <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\dot c$\">$\\dot c$</span> along a geodesic is parallel, and (by <a class=\"reference tex-ref\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI%23ex%3Apet-ch6-sphere-third-partials\" data-tex-node=\"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#ex:pet-ch6-sphere-third-partials\" data-tex-anchor=\"tex-label-65783a7065742d6368362d7370686572652d74686972642d7061727469616c73\">section 1</a>) the unit field perpendicular to a great circle in <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$S^2(1)$\">$S^2(1)$</span> is parallel. If <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$V,W$\">$V,W$</span> are parallel along <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$c$\">$c$</span> then <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$g(V,W)$\">$g(V,W)$</span> is constant along <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$c$\">$c$</span> by the product rule, so parallel fields neither change length nor relative angle, generalizing parallel translation in Euclidean space. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#lem:pet-ch6-third-partial-commutator","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-existence-uniqueness","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve","title":"def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve","label":"def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-existence-uniqueness","title":"thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-existence-uniqueness","label":"thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-existence-uniqueness"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-chart-local","title":"thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-chart-local","label":"thm:pet-ch6-parallel-field-chart-local"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-second-variation-special-cases","title":"rem:pet-ch6-second-variation-special-cases","label":"rem:pet-ch6-second-variation-special-cases"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-closed-parallel-field-construction","title":"rem:pet-ch6-closed-parallel-field-construction","label":"rem:pet-ch6-closed-parallel-field-construction"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-2","title":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-2","label":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-2"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-3","title":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-3","label":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-3"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-5","title":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-5","label":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-5"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-27","title":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-27","label":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-27"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-28","title":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-28","label":"rem:pet-ch6-ex-6-7-28"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","title":"def:pet-ch6-parallel-field","tex":"\\begin{definition}[parallel field along a curve]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch6-parallel-field}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0252}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve}\n\n  A vector field $V$ along $c$ is \\emph{parallel along $c$} if $\\dot V\\equiv0$.\n  The tangent field $\\dot c$ along a geodesic is parallel, and (by\n  \\cref{ex:pet-ch6-sphere-third-partials}) the unit field perpendicular to a\n  great circle in $S^2(1)$ is parallel. If $V,W$ are parallel along $c$ then\n  $g(V,W)$ is constant along $c$ by the product rule, so parallel fields neither\n  change length nor relative angle, generalizing parallel translation in\n  Euclidean space.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch06_SectionalCurvatureComparisonI#def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve","title":"def:pet-ch6-vector-field-along-curve","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}